The word 'learn' etymologically means to commit to memory — and memory consolidation literally requires sleep, making sleep biologically inseparable from learning.
NASA and the US military studied sleep deprivation after major incidents including the Chernobyl and Three Mile Island accidents, both of which involved sleep-deprived operators — sleep is a safety-critical issue.
REM sleep specifically consolidates emotional memories and creative connections; people woken during REM sleep cannot consolidate procedural skills (instrument playing, sports technique).
Medical residents historically worked 36-hour shifts; studies showed their diagnostic error rates were 36% higher under sleep deprivation than after adequate rest, leading to mandatory shift limits.
Memory impairment from sleep loss is not subjectively perceived — people believe they are performing fine even when objective tests show 20–40% degradation.
